Exacerbation of mild eczema patches on flexural surfaces triggered by seasonal temperature changes
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Pruritic flexural patches, Fine epidermal scaling, Worsening with cold/dry weather

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-006
Reason for Visit: Severe, unrelenting pruritus leading to significant skin excoriation and high risk of secondary bacterial skin infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated intensely pruritic skin lesions with active excoriation and bleeding. Urgent physical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to rule out superimposed staphylococcal infection and initiate immediate topical therapy to prevent further barrier breakdown and progression to widespread cellulitis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I can't stop scratching the bends of my arms and legs; the skin is raw and flaking off.
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with intensely pruritic erythematous patches in the antecubital and popliteal fossae, worsening progressively over the past 48 hours. Notes worsening of symptoms correlated with the recent onset of cold, dry weather. Patient reports fine dry epidermal scaling over affected areas and active excoriations secondary to recurrent scratching. Describes a personal history of atopy. Reports immediate blanching of erythema upon direct pressure, and a complete lack of vesicular or bullous formation.
Functional Status: Independent, but sleep significantly disrupted by pruritus.
Social/Safety Concerns: Sleep deprivation secondary to intense nocturnal itching.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/74 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 71 Kg
General: Alert, distressed by pruritus, actively scratching during interview.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals ill-defined poorly demarcated plaque borders with mild lichenification from chronic rubbing. Diagnostic triad confirmed: pruritic flexural patches, fine epidermal scaling, and a history of worsening with cold/dry weather. No fluctuance, no purulent drainage. Absence of targetoid lesions. Normal core temperature.
Environmental Observations: Central heating running constantly, drying ambient air.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Expired topical hydrocortisone found in bathroom cabinet.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Exacerbation of mild eczema patches on flexural surfaces triggered by seasonal temperature changes - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of honey-colored crusts or frank purulent exudate.

Scabies - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sparing of web spaces, absence of visible burrows, and isolated flexural distribution.

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral symmetrical flexural presentation and strong correlation with seasonal weather changes rather than a new contactant.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe exacerbation of atopic dermatitis leading to active excoriation and weeping of the epidermal barrier. Given the high risk of rapid progression to secondary staphylococcal or streptococcal infection due to continuous scratching, the assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period. Urgent intervention to break the itch-scratch cycle with appropriate topical corticosteroids and assess for occult early infection was required to prevent clinical deterioration.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescription for moderately potent topical corticosteroid ointment (e.g., betamethasone valerate 0.1%). Recommendation for heavy emollient application. Planned non-urgent serum IgE level testing.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply heavy emollients within 3 minutes of stepping out of a lukewarm shower to lock in moisture, reinforcing the habit by keeping the moisturizer directly adjacent to the towel.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to assess treatment response and step down steroid potency.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if the rash rapidly spreads, becomes hot and painful to touch, or if you develop a high fever and chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical exacerbation of a chronic condition; underlying allergic triggers may require further investigation.
Clinical Course: Pruritus should decrease significantly within 48 hours of starting the topical steroid, with skin healing following over 7 to 10 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the areas begin to weep yellow fluid, develop golden crusts, or if you feel systemically unwell.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The steroid ointment quickly reduces the severe inflammation causing the itch. Emollients repair the broken physical skin barrier, preventing moisture loss and blocking irritants.
Actionable Steps: Use lukewarm water for bathing. Apply steroid only to red, itchy areas. Apply thick moisturizers all over, multiple times daily.

